hadiths[11]{hadithnumber,text}:
"54","On the Friday sermon. Abu Tawbah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Aban ibn Abdullah, who said: I was with Adi ibn Thabit on Friday, and when the imam came out - or he said: ascended the minbar - he turned to face him and said: This is what the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"55","Ibn al-Sarh narrated to us, and Sulayman ibn Dawud narrated to us; Ibn Wahb informed us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, who said: It has reached us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would begin by sitting upon the minbar; when the mu'adhdhin fell silent, he stood and delivered the first sermon, then sat briefly, then stood and delivered the second sermon; when he finished it, he sought forgiveness, then descended and prayed. Ibn Shihab said: When he stood, he would take a staff and lean upon it while standing on the minbar; then Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, Umar ibn al-Khattab, and Uthman ibn Affan would do the same."
"56","Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from Uqayl, from al-Zuhri, who said: The opening of the sermon of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was: Praise be to Allah; we praise Him, seek His help, and seek His forgiveness. We seek refuge in Him from the evils of our souls. Whomever Allah guides, none can misguide, and whomever He sends astray, none can guide. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger; He sent him with the truth as a bearer of glad tidings and a warner before the Hour. Whoever obeys Allah and His Messenger is rightly guided, and whoever disobeys them has gone astray. I ask Allah our Lord to make us among those who obey Him, obey His Messenger, follow His pleasure, and avoid His wrath; for we exist only through Him and for Him."
"57","Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Muradi narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Yunus, that he asked Ibn Shihab about the tashahhud of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on Friday. Ibn Shihab said: Praise be to Allah; I praise Him and seek His help - then he mentioned the like of it exactly, meaning the like of the hadith of Uqayl."
"58","Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, who said: It has reached us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that when he preached he would say: All that is coming is near; what is coming is not far. Allah does not hasten for the haste of anyone, nor is He light for the sake of men's affairs. What Allah wills shall be, not what men will. Allah intends one thing and men intend another; what Allah wills comes to pass even if men dislike it. Nothing Allah has brought near can be distanced, and nothing He has distanced can be brought near. Nothing happens except by the permission of Allah, Majestic and Mighty."
"59","Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, Abu Imran al-Jawni informed us, from Abdullah ibn Rabah, from Ka'b al-Ahbar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Recite Hud on Friday. Abu Dawud said: Muslim said in this: From the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"60","Hannad ibn al-Sari narrated to us, from Abu Khalid al-Ahmar, from Hisham, from his father, who said: The most frequent words of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he sat upon the minbar were: Fear Allah and speak a right word."
"61","Qutaybah narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Sa'd, from Ibn Shihab, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat upon the minbar on Friday to supplicate, he would only point with his finger while the people said amen."
"62","Mahmud ibn Khalid narrated to us, al-Walid narrated to us; Abu Mu'adh Bukayr ibn Ma'ruf informed me that he heard Muqatil ibn Hayyan saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Friday before the sermon, like the two Eids, until one Friday, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was preaching - having already prayed Friday - a man entered and said: Dihyah ibn Khalifah has arrived with his merchandise; and when Dihyah arrived, his family would meet him with tambourines. So the people went out, thinking there was nothing wrong in leaving the sermon. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, then revealed: And when they see merchandise or amusement, they disperse toward it. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) put the Friday sermon first and delayed the prayer. No one would leave for a nosebleed or ritual impurity after the prohibition except by seeking the permission of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), gesturing to him with the finger next to the thumb, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would grant him permission by gesturing with his hand. Some hypocrites found the sermon and sitting in the mosque burdensome; when a Muslim man sought permission, the hypocrite beside him would rise, concealing himself with him, until he left. Then Allah, Majestic and Mighty, revealed: Allah surely knows those of you who slip away, concealing themselves - the verse."
"63","Hannad ibn al-Sari narrated to us, from Ibn al-Mubarak, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would speak between his descent from the minbar and his entry into prayer. Abu Dawud said: This has also been narrated with a connected chain, but it is not strong and is not authentic."
"64","Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Zayd, who said: Jarir ibn Hazim and I were with Thabit al-Bunani, when Hajjaj ibn Abi Uthman narrated, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the prayer is established, do not stand until you see me. So Jarir thought that Thabit had narrated it from Anas. In the Dar al-Sumay'i edition there is an unnumbered report after this hadith: Abu Dawud said: I heard Ahmad ibn Hanbal mention the hadith of Jarir ibn Hazim, from Thabit, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the prayer is established, do not stand until you see me. Ahmad said: They claim Hammad ibn Zayd said: Hajjaj ibn Abi Uthman narrated it to Thabit from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father; but Jarir thought Thabit narrated it from Anas, so he narrated it thus."
